+**BLOCK — revise before shipping: the title thesis still attributes a channel comparison to Lem that he did not make; the ledger contains an unfair `MISS`, an unsupported `EARLY`, two false 2026 formulations, and object/problem substitutions; and the essay still omits contract-required material.**
+
+# Consolidated review: Essay 6, “The cheap channel”
+
+Reviewer labels below are **G1–G14** for `06-grok.md` and **A1–A6** for `06-agy.md`. Corpus references were checked with `scripts/find.py`, including the full pp. 197–236 context. The quote gate itself is clean; the defects are interpretation, verdict mapping, coverage, and 2026 receipts.
+
+## 1. **CONFIRMED (G1):** the frame says Lem priced conversation, while p. 206 prices two full phantomats
+
+**Location:** metadata: “a channel he priced as the hard one: conversation”; lede and “What happened”: “the channel he priced as expensive”; versus “Where he was worst”: “he did not price the *channel*.”
+
+**Verification:** On pp. 202–203 and 206, both the “travel” and “human-producing” machines are bidirectional, nerve-connected phantomats. The latter adds an autonomous interlocutor inside the synthetic world. Lem says that complete device is harder than the travel phantomat; he never compares full-sensorium simulation with a text channel. The essay’s final critical paragraph reads the passage correctly, but its title frame, lede, metadata, and transition do not.
+
+**Fix:** Say that the essay—not Lem—transposes his detection game to a cheap text channel. Remove every formulation saying he priced conversation or the channel as expensive.
+
+## 2. **CONFIRMED (G2, A2):** the entertainment `MISS` is unfair and tests the wrong object
+
+**Location:** object row: “its entertainment role may turn out to be rather marginal” (p. 215) → `MISS`, because shipped headsets are entertainment-led.
+
+**Verification:** Lem hedges with “may.” More importantly, pp. 214–215 first say phantomatics “can mainly be a form of entertainment,” then list training, isolation, medicine, blindness, illness, and age before saying its entertainment role may be marginal. The essay’s own preceding row concedes that no headset is a phantomat. Quest’s use mix therefore cannot settle the use mix of Lem’s unbuilt machine.
+
+**Fix:** Make the phantomat application mix `OPEN`, quote both pp. 214 and 215, and keep headset use only as a clearly limited comparison. Agy’s alternative `DISSOLVED` is unsupported; the question remains meaningful if a phantomat is built.
+
+## 3. **CONFIRMED in core (G3), but the proposed replacement `MISS` is rejected:** after finding 2, the ledger has no defensible negative grade
+
+**Location:** “Where he was worst” says Lem priced the wrong scarce resource and assumed an interlocutor would be built rather than “grown from the record”; neither claim appears in the ledger.
+
+**Verification:** AGENT requires the ledger to contain the essay’s real `MISS` or wrong-answer row. Removing the entertainment row leaves none. G3 is right that the ledger and dinner test no longer encode the essay’s negative judgment.
+
+G3 overstates the available repair, however. The p. 206 ranking is still untested because neither phantomat exists. Page 203 says the machine must have complexity or intelligence matching the genius; it does not say a text chatbot must be constructed from an explicit model of that person. Current chatbots therefore do not establish an object-level `MISS` on Lem’s human-producing phantomat.
+
+**Fix:** Add a narrowly framed problem-level `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` only if the scored claim is Lem’s cost model for the detection problem and the text-channel transposition is explicitly the essay’s analogy. Do not turn p. 203 into a failed object prediction. If no specific negative row survives that standard, revise the thesis or explain honestly why the chapter differs under AGENT’s rule.
+
+## 4. **CONFIRMED — missed by both reviewers:** “built, from an understanding of the person” is not in Lem
+
+**Location:** “He also assumed the interlocutor would have to be *built*, from an understanding of the person.”
+
+**Verification:** Pages 202–203 propose a general program, memory banks containing facts, and “appropriately designed self-organizing systems.” They require relative autonomy and sufficient complexity or intelligence. They do not require an explicit theory or “understanding of the person.” The narrower omission—that the chapter never proposes training general competence on a text corpus at scale—is supportable; the stronger construction claim is invented.
+
+**Fix:** Replace the sentence with the precise omission. Do not score it as a direct contradiction unless a specific paged Lem claim and matching present object can be stated.
+
+## 5. **CONFIRMED (G4):** Bostrom does not formalize Lem’s p. 207 conclusion
+
+**Location:** prose: Lem’s “only probable, never certain” “became, in Bostrom’s 2003 paper, the simulation argument”; problem row: “formalises the same conclusion” → `HIT`.
+
+**Verification:** Lem gives an indistinguishability/brain-in-a-vat argument: a sufficiently complete phantomat can defeat every test. Bostrom gives a population-based empirical trilemma about posthuman ancestor simulations. Bostrom explicitly says his argument is “fundamentally different” from brain-in-a-vat skepticism and that its conclusion is not simply uncertainty about being simulated. [Bostrom’s FAQ, question 3](https://simulation-argument.com/faq/)
+
+**Fix:** Remove Bostrom as the receipt for “the same conclusion.” Use the live skeptical literature for a problem-level `HIT`, or make Bostrom a related but distinct descendant with its own claim.
+
+## 6. **CONFIRMED for teletaxy; REJECTED for head tracking (G5, A3):** the remote-I row launders an absent object, but the p. 202 row does not
+
+**Location:** teletaxy: nerve-linked remote-I → transatlantic telesurgery → `RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON`; neck-muscle countering → external head tracking → the same verdict.
+
+**Verification:** Page 222 specifies a human double whose sensory receptors and motor nerves are connected to the operator. The Lindbergh operation used a robot, displays, and hand controls. That is not the predicted object, so its real latency constraint cannot pay an object-level match.
+
+The reviewers’ extension to the p. 202 row is wrong. There the functional outcome being scored is that intended head movement changes the rendered field; external tracking achieved that outcome through a different mechanism. `RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON` is exactly the permitted verdict for an outcome match with a mechanism miss, provided the row names that outcome precisely.
+
+**Fix:** Split teletaxy into an object row (`OPEN` unless a dated nerve-linked path is receipted) and a problem row (light-speed delay bounds teleoperation → `HIT`). Keep the head-tracking row, but word the Lem cell as the functional outcome.
+
+## 7. **CONFIRMED (G6):** full phantomatics cannot be `EARLY` when the essay admits there is no dated path
+
+**Location:** “Phantomatics proper: bidirectional, no exits, indistinguishable” → `EARLY`; receipt: “no dated path on the efferent, no-exits side”; still-open: “Whether the travel phantomat arrives at all.”
+
+**Verification:** CONTEXT defines `EARLY` as a live, dated path. Half Dome is a dated path to accommodation, not to full bidirectionality, “no exits,” or indistinguishability. The row’s evidence and the still-open section both concede that gap.
+
+**Fix:** Make the full object `OPEN`; split accommodation/input-side work into a narrower `EARLY` row if useful.
+
+## 8. **CONFIRMED (G7); A6 is rejected:** three 2026 formulations are false or unsupported by their receipts
+
+**Location and verification:**
+
+- “the first commercial implants … Neuralink’s first human participant” is false. N1 is an **investigational** device in a clinical trial, not a commercial implant. [Neuralink’s trial page](https://neuralink.com/trials/device-control/)
+- “the latency budget he computed for the Moon is the one operators still work to” is not paid by `lindbergh-2001`. Lem gives about one second each way; the 2001 operation achieved roughly 155 ms, and the same team estimated about 300 ms as the safe ceiling. A lunar loop remains too slow. [Marescaux et al.](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1422462/)
+- “First vitrified mammalian organ rewarmed and transplanted, 2023” is false. A vitrified rabbit kidney was transplanted and supported life before the 2009 report. Han et al. 2023 supplied the first **reproducible** long-term rat result and explicitly acknowledge the earlier single success. [Fahy et al. 2009](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2781097/), [Han et al. 2023](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-023-38824-8)
+
+**Fix:** Say “investigational implant”; invert the latency point (“the lunar delay remains disqualifying”); and describe 2023 as reproducible rat-kidney vitrification, storage, nanowarming, and life-sustaining transplantation—not the first mammalian organ.
+
+A6’s blanket claim that every 2026 statement is marked and accurate is therefore **REJECTED**. Its Reality Labs aside does not validate the rest and describes the total as a projection even though the local row cites a July 2026 report.
+
+## 9. **CONFIRMED (G8):** Guess 2023 does not support “best 2026 evidence,” and “short exposure” contradicts the receipt
+
+**Location:** “That is roughly where the best 2026 evidence sits” followed by a three-month chronological-feed intervention; gloss: “Short exposure.”
+
+**Verification:** Three months is not “short” on the essay’s own description and is at least a plausible instance of Lem’s “prolonged period.” Guess et al. changed feed ranking; it did not reproduce Lem’s claim about identical inputs being interpreted differently or his example of deliberately staged repeated wickedness. It supports only a narrow null result for that Meta intervention. A 2026 randomized X study found that seven weeks of switching an algorithmic feed on shifted several political opinions, so the selected null result cannot stand for “the best 2026 evidence.” [Gauthier et al., *Nature* 2026](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10098-2)
+
+**Fix:** Present Guess as one platform-specific null result, add the contrary 2026 evidence, and remove “Short exposure” and “best.”
+
+## 10. **CONFIRMED in core; reviewer analogies partly rejected (G9):** the essay still fails its chapter contract
+
+**Location:** central phantomatics appears only as “Olds and Milner’s rats”; deepfakes and the Superphantomat mechanism are absent; cerebromatics is narrated but has no ledger row.
+
+**Verification:** CONTEXT explicitly requires peripheral versus central phantomatics, deepfakes, BCI, and personality/information. Page 210 defines central phantomatics as direct stimulation of reward/punishment centers and a “technology of facilitated ecstasy.” Pages 207–208 make the Superphantomat a reductio. The essay does not test either, omits deepfakes, and does not score p. 216’s claim that absent traits cannot be inserted.
+
+G9’s statement that feeds are “central phantomatics” is **REJECTED**: feeds act through ordinary sensory input and fit Lem’s peripheral, staged-exposure discussion, not direct brain-center stimulation. Its suggested DBS-plus-engagement row would mix mechanisms. “Cerebromatics against BCI write” also needs actual write-side evidence; Neuralink’s motor read-out cannot pay it.
+
+**Fix:** Add distinct, honestly receipted rows for central stimulation, cerebromatic trait/skill insertion, and deepfakes as a cheap unidirectional detection problem. Treat the Superphantomat only as a reductio mechanism, per AGENT.
+
+## 11. **CONFIRMED in part; proposed verdict rejected (G10):** the Parfit row joins two claims and the prose turns comparison into applause
+
+**Location:** one row joins “copy is not the original” with “telegraphing … remains out of the question” → `OPEN`; prose: “Parfit needed the case where the original survives” and Lem’s version is “better engineered.”
+
+**Verification:** The row combines a claim about numerical identity with a practical/normative conclusion about survival. Parfit’s Part III develops the rival view that identity is not what matters. *Reasons and Persons* introduces simple Teletransportation and then the Branch-Line Case; the surviving-original case is an added comparison, not the premise without which he could begin. [Book contents and chapter structure](https://academic.oup.com/book/12484), [Stanford Encyclopedia on Parfit’s view](https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2020/entries/identity-ethics/)
+
+G10 is right to demand a split and to cut “better engineered.” Its proposed `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` is **REJECTED**: the existence of a Parfitian rival does not settle the philosophical question. `OPEN` remains appropriate for the disputed answer once the two claims are separated.
+
+**Fix:** Split identity, survival, and practical permissibility. Describe Lem and Parfit as parallel constructions with different conclusions; do not grade prose with “better.”
+
+## 12. **CONFIRMED in core / UNVERIFIABLE in its date-specific form (G11):** later Lem is invoked and then exempted from the book’s scoring rule
+
+**Location:** “Zylinska summarises the 1982 and 1991 afterwords … Eye Phone and Data Glove … this essay does not score them.”
+
+**Verification:** Corpus p. 21 says the later essays updated *Summa* with scientific equivalents including EyePhone and DataGlove, with the aim of showing how much Lem predicted correctly. AGENT says later self-grades must be scored. The essay cannot use that summary as evidence and then decline to assess it solely because the afterwords are absent.
+
+G11’s label “1991-Lem” is **UNVERIFIABLE from this corpus**: Zylinska pools the 1982 and 1991 essays and does not assign those devices to one of them. The exact wording and scope of Lem’s self-grade are likewise unavailable.
+
+**Fix:** Add a carefully limited row attributed to Zylinska’s summary and grade only what the summary establishes, or obtain the afterwords. Preserve the corpus-gap caveat.
+
+## 13. **CONFIRMED on layer; reviewer’s verdict is rejected (G12, A4):** the “neurotics” event is misplaced and the receipt proves only reporting
+
+**Location:** problem-level row: phantomat users in psychiatric waiting rooms → 2025 newspaper reports about chatbot-associated derealization → `RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON`.
+
+**Verification:** A predicted clinical/social occurrence is object-level. More seriously, AGENT says reports are not the event. The receipt establishes that newspapers reported cases; it does not clinically establish the causal outcome Lem predicted. The essay also substitutes chatbots for phantomats.
+
+**Fix:** Object-level `OPEN` for the phantomat prediction. If the transposed chatbot concern remains at problem level, state it as reported evidence and use `OPEN` or `EARLY`; do not award `RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON` merely for “same waiting room.”
+
+## 14. **CONFIRMED (G13):** present-tense claims are unmarked or ride on rows that do not support them
+
+**Location:**
+
+- “stimulation is still therapeutic modulation, not the insertion of a skill” is not supported by `neuralink-2024`;
+- the lunar “latency budget” is not in `lindbergh-2001`;
+- “commercial implants” contradicts `neuralink-2024`;
+- “Meta’s losses are the current price of the experiment” is repeated in Still open without a marker;
+- “from which today’s consumer headsets descend” is not in `oculus-2012`.
+
+**Fix:** Add claim rows with sources or cut/narrow the statements. A marker after a compound paragraph does not receipt assertions absent from its TSV row.
+
+## 15. **CONFIRMED as a consequence, not a separate defect (G14, A5):** the ledger fails the skeptic/dinner test
+
+The reader problem follows from findings 1–14: the persuasive negative argument lives in prose, while the ledger awards Bostrom, headset use, telesurgery, and reported chatbot cases more credit than their receipts earn. Rebuild the rows first.
+
+A5’s general rule that any missed mechanism must be `EARLY` or `MISS` is **REJECTED**. `RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON` exists precisely for an outcome that arrives through a different mechanism; the p. 202 head-tracking row is a legitimate example when narrowly written.
+
+## 16. **REJECTED (A1):** Lem does not present phantomat censorship as an easy solution to alignment
+
+A1 quotes p. 212’s setup—controls “seem” purely technical and probably not difficult—but omits the immediate reversal on pp. 212–213. Lem explains that the censor cannot know the subjective context: identical stimuli can be martyrdom, masochism, pain, or pleasure, and phantomatics cannot directly determine attitudes, beliefs, or feelings. That is the problem he is exposing, not a confident resolution. Generative-model jailbreaking is also a different object from censoring a patron’s conduct inside a phantomat.
+
+**Fix:** Do not add A1’s proposed `MISS`. If the passage is used, score Lem’s argument about the limits of behavioral censorship as an argument, with a genuinely matched 2026 comparator.
+
+## 17. **CONFIRMED — missed by both reviewers:** the Still-open closure test does not close Lem’s definition
+
+**Location:** “What would close it: a display that solves accommodation and haptics well enough for a majority of users to keep it on for hours, and a market that pays for it.”
+
+**Verification:** Comfort, haptics, duration, and market adoption do not establish bidirectional nerve interaction, “no exits,” or indistinguishability. A commercially successful headset could meet every listed criterion and remain the “circarama with a strap” that the essay says Lem excludes.
+
+**Fix:** Define closure using the actual scored claim: a system that defeats adversarial reality tests while providing bidirectional full-sensorium interaction. Treat adoption as a separate product question.
+
+## 18. **CONFIRMED — missed by both reviewers:** the essay’s load-bearing bandwidth numbers are unreceipted
+
+**Location:** “A world is gigabits a second”; “a conversation is a few bytes a second.”
+
+**Verification:** These quantitative claims carry no `CHECK` marker. They are also not self-defining: “world” could mean retinal input, rendered video, sensory nerve traffic, or encoded display data; conversation could mean characters, audio, semantic content, or model computation. The chosen quantities make the title argument sound measured when it is only a metaphor.
+
+**Fix:** Define and source comparable channel measures, or remove the numbers and make the qualitative asymmetry explicit.
+
+## 19. **CONFIRMED — missed by both reviewers:** the metadata overstates Parfit as philosophy’s first arrival
+
+**Location:** “the argument about copying and rewriting people that philosophy reached twenty years later.”
+
+**Verification:** The essay itself identifies Wiener’s earlier telegraphing proposal, and Lem’s pages are already a philosophical identity argument. Parfit published on personal identity in the early 1970s before the 1984 book. “Reached twenty years later” can only mean that one famous book used related cases, not that philosophy first reached the problem then. [Stanford Encyclopedia](https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2020/entries/identity-ethics/)
+
+**Fix:** Say that Lem anticipated the structure of cases later made canonical by Parfit’s 1984 book.
+
+## Reviewer-by-reviewer disposition
